+The x86 mapper projects a six-byte opcode record and shared implicit-operand
+records from `X86MappingInsn*.inc` with
+`suite/synctools/x86_mapping_counts.py`. Opcodes retain their sorted internal
+ID, public ID and 64-bit flag. Opcodes with the same implicit registers, groups,
+jump classification and repair eligibility can share a detail record. The
+register and group counts stop at the first zero. The mapper copies the full
+source arrays, including entries beyond the counts. Bit 14 of the count word
+selects the public-ID repair switches in `X86_get_insn_id`. DIET reads its
+opcode records directly from the canonical tables.
 Run the generator after changing either mapping table or the repair switches.
 Its `--check` mode and unit checks validate the full/reduced projection and reject
 statements that cannot be skipped under this guard.
